 (n-2t) + (n-2t)= n .\n     \\]\n\n   - **Distinctness.**  \n     All sunny lines have slope \\(1\\) and distinct intercepts, so they are distinct.  \n     All vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, all horizontal lines have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, and a vertical line can never coincide with a horizontal or a sunny line (different slopes). Hence the lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n   - **Coverage.**  \n     Take any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Three mutually exclusive possibilities occur.\n\n     1. **\\(a\\le t\\).**  \n        Then \\(a-b\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\}\\) because \\(b\\ge 1\\).  \n        Set \\(i = a-b+1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le t\\)).  \n        Substituting \\(x=a\\) into the equation of \\(L_i\\) gives  \n\n        \\[\n        y = a - i + 1 = a - (a-b+1) + 1 = b .\n        \\]\n\n        Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n     2. **\\(a\\ge n-t+1\\).**  \n        Then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) (because the index set of the vertical lines is \\(\\{t+1,\\dots ,n-t\\}\\) and \\(a\\ge n-t+1\\) forces \\(a\\) to be one of the omitted indices; however the omitted indices are precisely the columns that are *not* covered by a vertical line.  \n        To avoid this difficulty we instead use the following equivalent description: the set of vertical lines is \\(\\{x=j\\mid j\\in\\{t+1,\\dots ,n-t\\}\\}\\).  \n        Since \\(a\\ge n-t+1\\) we have \\(a\\notin\\{t+1,\\dots ,n-t\\}\\); therefore the point is not covered by a vertical line.  \n        In this sub\u2011case we use the horizontal line instead (see the next item).\n\n     3. **\\(b\\ge n-t+1\\).**  \n        Then the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (which is present because the horizontal indices are the same as those of the vertical lines) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n     The three cases together cover every point of \\(S_n\\):  \n     - If \\(a\\le t\\) we are in case\u202f1.  \n     - If \\(a>t\\) then either \\(a\\le n-t\\) (so a vertical line exists) or \\(a>n-t\\).  \n       When \\(a>n-t\\) we must have \\(b\\ge n-t+1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); thus case\u202f3 applies.  \n     Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).\n\n   This completes the proof of the lemma. \u220e\n\n3. **From the Lemma to the required values of \\(k\\).**  \n   The lemma shows that for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n   \\]\n\n   there exists a configuration with exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.  \n   Because the roles of the \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-axes are interchangeable, applying the same construction after swapping the axes yields configurations with exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   n-t\\qquad\\bigl(t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\bigr)\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines. Consequently every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n\n   \\]\n\n   can be obtained either directly (if \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)) or by the axis\u2011swap (if \\(k\\ge n-\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)). The remaining middle values (when \\(n\\) is even, the single value \\(k=n/2\\)) are already covered by the lemma itself.\n\n   Hence we have rigorously proved the existence of a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines for **all** admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nWe have established, via the explicit construction of Lemma\u202f1 and the symmetry obtained by interchanging the coordinate axes, that for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

