ation is carried out in the detailed proof.\n\n4. **From the Lemma to the answer.**  \n   Because the lemma works for *every* \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\),\n   the required configuration exists for each such \\(k\\).  \n   Hence the admissible set of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\nConsequently the following three families are *non\u2011sunny*:\n\n* vertical lines\u2003\\(V_j:\\;x=j\\)\u2003(\\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\));\n* horizontal lines\u2003\\(H_j:\\;y=j\\)\u2003(\\(j\\in\\mathbb Z\\));\n* diagonal lines\u2003\\(D_s:\\;x+y=s\\)\u2003(\\(s\\in\\mathbb Z\\)).\n\nA line of slope \\(1\\) is sunny; we write it as  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x-c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma (mixed construction)\n\n*Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nDefine*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nv&=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\\\[2mm]\nh&=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\\\[2mm]\nd&=n-v-h-k\\qquad(\\text{so }d\\ge 0),\\\\[2mm]\ns&=k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Consider the family*\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\underbrace{\\{V_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,v\\}}_{\\text{vertical}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{H_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,h\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{D_{v+h+r}\\mid r=1,\\dots ,d\\}}_{\\text{diagonal}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{L_{v+h+d+r}\\mid r=1,\\dots ,s\\}}_{\\text{sunny}} .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Then*\n\n1. *\\(|\\mathcal F| = v+h+d+s = n\\); exactly \\(s=k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal F\\) are sunny (the lines \\(L_c\\)).*\n2. *Every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\).*\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Proof of the Lemma\n\n---\n\n**(1) Number of lines and sunny count.**  \nBy construction the four sub\u2011families contain respectively \\(v\\), \\(h\\), \\(d\\) and \\(s\\) distinct lines; their union therefore has  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal F| = v+h+d+s = n .\n\\]\n\nThe only sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) are the \\(L_c\\)\u2019s, and there are exactly \\(s=k\\) of them.\n\nAll lines are distinct because each sub\u2011family has a different slope\n(\\(0,\\infty,-1,1\\)), hence no line can belong to two different sub\u2011families.\n\n---\n\n**(2) Coverage of \\(S_n\\).**  \nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\); thus  \n\n\\[\na\\ge 1,\\qquad b\\ge 1,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe distinguish three mutually exclusive cases.\n\n---\n\n**Case\u202fA \u2013 \\(a\\le v\\).**  \nThen the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which belongs to the first sub\u2011family) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case\u202fB \u2013 \\(b\\le h\\).**  \nThen the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (second sub\u2011family) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case\u202fC \u2013 \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\).**  \nFrom (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n(a+b)-(v+h)\\le n+1-(v+h).\n\\]\n\nBecause of the definitions of \\(v\\) and \\(h\\),\n\n\\[\nn-(v+h)=d+s .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n(a+b)-(v+h)\\le d+s . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nt:=a+b-(v+h) .\n\\]\n\nEquation (3) tells us that \\(1\\le t\\le d+s\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le t\\le d\\)*, then the diagonal line \\(D_{v+h+t}\\) (third sub\u2011family) contains \\((a,b)\\) because its equation is \\(x+y=v+h+t\\).\n\n*If \\(d+1\\le t\\le d+s\\)*, write \\(t=d+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le s\\).  \nThen the sunny line \\(L_{v+h+d+r}\\) (fourth sub\u2011family) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny = x-(v+h+d+r).\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = a-(v+h+d+r)=a-(v+h+d)+( -r)=a-(v+h+d)-r .\n\\]\n\nBut from the definition of \\(t\\),\n\n\\[\