is omission is fatal because the construction provides lines only for \\(t\\) in the range \\(1\\le t\\le d\\) (diagonal lines) **or** \\(d+1\\le t\\le d+s\\) (sunny lines). Since \\(d=0\\), the only available lines correspond to \\(1\\le t\\le s=k\\). The case \\(t=k+1\\) is **not** covered by any line in \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\n**Consequence:** Points with \\(a>v\\), \\(b>h\\) and \\(a+b = n+1\\) (i.e. \\(t=k+1\\)) are left uncovered. A concrete counterexample is \\((a,b)=(2,2)\\) for \\(n=3, k=1\\): here \\(v=1\\), \\(h=1\\), \\(t=2\\), \\(k=1\\), so \\(t=k+1\\); the constructed family \\(\\{x=1, y=1, y=x-3\\}\\) does not contain \\((2,2)\\).\n\n#### 6.4 Sub‑cases based on the (incorrect) bound\n\n> “*If \\(1\\le t\\le d\\)*, then the diagonal line \\(D_{v+h+t}\\) (third sub‑family) contains \\((a,b)\\) because its equation is \\(x+y=v+h+t\\).  \n> *If \\(d+1\\le t\\le d+s\\)*, write \\(t=d+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le s\\). Then the sunny line \\(L_{v+h+d+r}\\) (fourth sub‑family) has equation …”\n\n**Verification:** The logical structure of the sub‑cases is sound **provided** the bound \\(1\\le t\\le d+s\\) holds for all points in Case C. Because the bound is false for points with \\(t=k+1\\), the argument does not address those points. Moreover, since \\(d=0\\), the first sub‑case never occurs, and the second sub‑case only covers \\(t\\) up to \\(k\\). Hence the construction fails to cover the points with \\(t=k+1\\).\n\n**Critical Error:** The proof assumes the existence of diagonal lines (the \\(d\\)‑family) to handle part of Case C, but \\(d\\) is always zero, so no such lines exist. The missing case \\(t=k+1\\) is not handled, rendering the coverage claim false.\n\n#### 6.5 Conclusion of the Lemma\n\n> “In every sub‑case of Case C the point belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal F\\). Since the three cases exhaust all possibilities, every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered. ∎”\n\n**Verification:** The conclusion is invalid because the exhaustive claim is based on the erroneous bound (3). As demonstrated, there exist points in \\(S_n\\) that are not covered by any line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) for many admissible \\((n,k)\\). Therefore the Lemma is false.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Completion of the problem\n\n> “For the given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we apply the Lemma … Therefore **every** integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable.”\n\n**Verification:** This conclusion directly relies on the Lemma, which has been shown to be incorrect. Consequently the final answer \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is not justified. In fact, the construction fails for many values of \\(k\\) (e.g., \\(n=3, k=1\\)), so the claimed set of attainable \\(k\\) is false.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution’s core construction collapses because the parameter \\(d\\) is identically zero, yet the proof of coverage crucially depends on the presence of diagonal lines (the \\(d\\)-family) and on an incorrect bound for the quantity \\(t\\). This leads to uncovered points in \\(S_n\\) for many choices of \\(k\\). Hence the solution contains a **Critical Error** that invalidates the entire argument. No other steps compensate for this flaw. The answer is therefore **incorrect**."
