).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Vertical lines.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_r=\\bigl(n-k+r,\\;k+1-r\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Because  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k+r)+(k+1-r)=n+1,\n   \\]\n\n   each \\(P_r\\) lies on the hypotenuse of the triangle \\(S_n\\) and therefore belongs to \\(S_n\\).\n   Through \\(P_r\\) draw the line of slope \\(1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   L_r:\\;y=x-c_r,\\qquad \n   c_r:=P_r^{(a)}-P_r^{(b)}=(n-k+r)-(k+1-r)=2(n-k+r)-(n+1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The lines \\(L_r\\) are sunny because their slope equals \\(1\\).\n\n3. **Family of lines.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k:=\\{V_j\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\cup\\{L_r\\mid r=1,\\dots ,k\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The two sub\u2011families have distinct slopes, hence all lines in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) are distinct.\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal F_k|=(n-k)+k=n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_r\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma (coverage)\n\n*Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \nThen the vertical line \\(V_a\\) from (1) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a>n-k\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k\\).  \nDefine \\(\\Delta:=a-b\\).\n\nFor the sunny line \\(L_r\\) we have, by (2),\n\n\\[\nL_r=\\{(x,y)\\mid x-y=c_r\\},\n\\qquad\nc_r=2(n-k+r)-(n+1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(r\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\), the constants \\(c_r\\) form the set  \n\n\\[\nC:=\\{\\,n-2k+1,\\;n-2k+3,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a>n-k\\),\n\n\\[\n\\Delta=a-b\\ge a-(n+1-