pe \\(\\infty\\) and the \\(L_r\\) have slope \\(1\\); within each sub‑family the parameters (\\(j\\) or \\(c_r\\)) are distinct, so all \\(n\\) lines are distinct. The count and the number of sunny lines are correct.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Lemma (coverage) – statement\n\n> “*Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).*”\n\n**Verification:** This is the crucial claim that must be proved.\n\n---\n\n### 14. Proof – Case 1\n\n> “*Case 1: \\(a\\le n-k\\).*  \n> Then the vertical line \\(V_a\\) from (1) contains \\((a,b)\\).”\n\n**Verification:** Correct. If the \\(x\\)-coordinate of the point is at most \\(n-k\\), the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is among the constructed lines and contains the point.\n\n---\n\n### 15. Proof – Case 2 (initial steps)\n\n> “*Case 2: \\(a>n-k\\).*  \n> From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le k\\).  \n> Define \\(\\Delta:=a-b\\).”\n\n**Verification:** Since \\(a>n-k\\) implies \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), we have \\(n+1-a\\le k\\); thus \\(b\\le k\\). The definition \\(\\Delta=a-b\\) is fine.\n\n---\n\n### 16. Representation of sunny lines\n\n> “For the sunny line \\(L_r\\) we have, by (2),  \n> \\[\n> L_r=\\{(x,y)\\mid x-y=c_r\\},\n> \\qquad\n> c_r=2(n-k+r)-(n+1).\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:** Correct rewriting of the line equation.\n\n---\n\n### 17. Set of constants \\(c_r\\)\n\n> “When \\(r\\) runs from \\(1\\) to \\(k\\), the constants \\(c_r\\) form the set  \n> \\[\n> C:=\\{\\,n-2k+1,\\;n-2k+3,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}.\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:** Substituting \\(r=1\\) gives \\(c_1=n-2k+1\\); substituting \\(r=k\\) gives \\(c_k=n-1\\). The step of \\(2\\) between successive \\(c_r\\) yields exactly the listed arithmetic progression. Hence the description of \\(C\\) is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 18. Bounds for \\(\\Delta\\)\n\n> “Because \\(a>n-k\\),  \n> \\[\n> \\Delta=a-b\\ge a-(n+1-a)=2a-(n+1)\\ge 2(n-k+1)-(n+1)=n-2k+1,\n> \\]  \n> and trivially \\(\\Delta\\le a-1\\le n-1\\).  \n> Thus \\(\\Delta\\in[\\,n-2k+1,\\;n-1\\,]\\).”\n\n**Verification:** The inequalities are algebraically correct:\n- From \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) we get \\(\\Delta\\ge 2a-(n+1)\\).\n- Since \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), the lower bound becomes \\(n-2k+1\\).\n- The upper bound \\(\\Delta\\le a-1\\) follows from \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a\\le n\\). Hence the interval inclusion is valid.\n\n---\n\n### 19. **Critical Claim (erroneous)**\n\n> “All integers in this interval belong to the set \\(C\\) (5); consequently there exists a unique \\(r\\) with \\(\\Delta=c_r\\).”\n\n**Verification:** **Critical Error.**  \nThe set \\(C\\) consists of numbers of the form \\(n-2k+1+2(r-1)\\); i.e., all numbers in the interval that have the **same parity** as \\(n-2k+1\\). The interval \\([\\,n-2k+1,\\;n-1\\,]\\) contains both parities whenever its length exceeds \\(1\\) (which occurs for any \\(k\\ge2\\)). Consequently many admissible values of \\(\\Delta\\) are **not** in \\(C\\).  \n\n*Concrete counterexample:*  \nTake \\(n=5,\\;k=2\\). Then \\(n-2k+1=2\\) and \\(C=\\{2,4\\}\\).  \nConsider the point \\((a,b)=(4,1)\\in S_5\\) (since \\(4+1=5\\le6\\)). Here \\(\\Delta=4-1=3\\), which lies in the interval \\([2,4]\\) but **not** in \\(C\\). Hence \\((4,1)\\) is not covered by any line of \\(\\mathcal F_2\\) (the vertical lines are \\(x=1,2,3\\); the sunny lines are \\(x-y=2\\) and \\(x-y=4\\)). This contradicts the Lemma.  \n\nTherefore the assertion that every \\(\\Delta\\) belongs to \\(C\\) is false, and the Lemma fails. Consequently the construction does **not** guarantee coverage of all points of \\(S_n\\) for many values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 20. Consequence of the error\n\nBecause the Lemma is false, the claim “for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\)” is unsupported. In fact, the construction fails for several admissible pairs \\((n,k)\\) (e.g., \\((5,2)\\) as shown). Hence the existence part of the solution is incorrect.\n\n---\n\n### 21. Converse argument\n\n> “Conversely, among \\(n\\) distinct lines we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, so no integer \\(k>n\\) is possible.”\n\n**Verification:** This statement is trivially true (you cannot have more sunny lines than total lines). However, it does not compensate for the failure of the existence construction. The overall answer (that all \\(k\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\) are attainable) is therefore false.\n\n---\n\n### 22. Overall assessment\n\nThe solution correctly defines the problem’s objects and constructs a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. The counting and distinctness arguments are sound. The fatal flaw lies in the coverage Lemma: the parity mismatch between \\(\\Delta=a-b\\) and the set of constants \\(c_r\\) means many points of \\(S_n\\) are left uncovered. This invalidates the central existence claim, rendering the solution incorrect. No further steps can salvage the result.\n\n---"
