ne contains three points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\) then two of those points have the same\n\\(x\\)-coordinate or the same \\(y\\)-coordinate or satisfy \\(a+b=\\text{constant}\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{same }x \\Longrightarrow \\text{slope } \\infty,\\\\\n&\\text{same }y \\Longrightarrow \\text{slope } 0,\\\\\n&\\text{same }a+b \\Longrightarrow \\text{slope } -1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll three possibilities are non‑sunny, contradicting the hypothesis. ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is vertical (\\(x=c\\)), the only possible values of \\(c\\) are \\(1,2,3\\); the\ncorresponding columns contain respectively three, two and one points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).\nIf the line is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), the possible values are \\(c=1,2,3\\) with\nthree, two and one points.  \nIf the line has slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=d\\)), the possible values of \\(d\\) are\n\\(2,3,4,5\\); the sets \\(\\{(1,1)\\},\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\},\\varnothing\\)\ncontain at most three points. ∎\n\n\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that three distinct lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) cover\n\\(\\mathcal{P}\\) and exactly two of them are sunny.  \nWithout loss of generality let \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) be sunny and \\(L_{3}\\) non‑sunny.\n\nBy Lemma 1 each of \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\);\nhence together they cover at most four points.  \nConsequently \\(L_{3}\\) must contain the remaining two (or three) points.\n\nWe now examine all possibilities for the direction of \\(L_{3}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  \\(L_{3}\\) vertical, \\(x=c\\).\n\n*If \\(c=1\\):* \\(L_{3}\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe four points left are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nAny line through two of them is either vertical (\\(x=2\\)), horizontal (\\(y=1\\)),\nor has slope \\(-1\\) (the line through \\((2,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\)).  \nAll are non‑sunny, contradicting that \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) are