umn \\(a\\) that are covered by\n   sunny lines.  Because each sunny line contributes at most one point to a\n   column,  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le r_a\\le k\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n   \\sum_{a=1}^{n} r_a\\le kn . \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The uncovered points in column \\(a\\) equal \\((n+1-a)-r_a\\).\n   If this number is positive, the column needs **at least one** non\u2011sunny\n   line (otherwise the uncovered points could not be covered, because a\n   horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line can supply at most one point per column,\n   while a vertical line can cover only a single column).  Hence the\n   number \\(N_{\\text{ns}}\\) of non\u2011sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   N_{\\text{ns}}\\ge\n   \\bigl|\\{a\\mid (n+1-a)-r_a>0\\}\\bigr| . \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   A column can be completely covered by sunny lines only when\n   \\(n+1-a\\le k\\); i.e. only for columns with  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\ge n+1-k . \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus at most \\(k\\) columns can be fully covered by sunny lines, and at\n   least \\(n-k\\) columns require a non\u2011sunny line.  From (4.2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   N_{\\text{ns}}\\ge n-k . \\tag{4.4}\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of lines equals  \n\n   \\[\n   k+N_{\\text{ns}}\\ge k+(n-k)=n . \\tag{4.5}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) then \\(n-k\\ge2\\), and (4.4) gives\n   \\(N_{\\text{ns}}\\ge n-k\\ge2\\).  Substituting into (4.5) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k+N_{\\text{ns}}\\ge n+1 ,\n   \\]\n\n   contradicting the hypothesis that exactly \\(n\\) lines are used.\n   Therefore no configuration exists with \\(k\\) in the interval\n   \\([2,n-1]\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,n\\) with the impossibility\nproof for all other values, we obtain the complete description of the\nadmissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
