he \\(n-k\\) columns \\(a\\le n-k\\) are covered by vertical lines and **no** horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line is used.  \n   In that extremal situation each of the remaining \\(k\\) columns \\(a=n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\) must be completely covered by sunny lines.  \n   Because a sunny line contributes at most one point to a column, the \\(k\\) sunny lines together can supply at most \\(k\\) points to each of those columns.  \n   The column \\(a=n\\) contains a single point \\((n,1)\\); therefore one sunny line must pass through \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Any line through \\((n,1)\\) and another point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a<n\\) has slope\n   \\[\n   \\frac{b-1}{a-n}.\n   \\]\n   For the line to be sunny this slope must be different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   The only way to avoid slope \\(-1\\) is to choose a point with \\(b\\neq n-a+1\\).  \n   But every point of the set \\(S_n\\) with \\(a<n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n-a\\); hence \\(b=n-a\\) is the **largest** possible ordinate in column \\(a\\).  \n   The point \\((a,n-a)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\).  \n   Consequently any sunny line that contains \\((n,1)\\) must also contain a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-a-1\\).  \n   Such a point belongs to a column \\(a\\le n-1\\) that, by (4.2), already requires a vertical line for full coverage.  \n   Hence the sunny line would duplicate a point already covered by a vertical line, leaving at least one point of the right\u2011most column uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n   Therefore the equality case cannot occur, and we must have\n   \\[\n   k+N_{\\text{ns}} > n .\n   \\]\n   Since exactly \\(n\\) lines are allowed, the assumption \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n   The only remaining possibilities are \\(k=0,\\,k=1\\) and \\(k=n\\), all of which have been exhibited in items\u202f1\u20133.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining the explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,n\\) with the impossibility p