ument that \\(i\\) is always an integer is unfounded.  \n\n**Classification:** This is a **Justification Gap**. The step’s conclusion may be true for some pairs \\((a,b)\\) but is not proved for all required pairs.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Impossibility proof – preliminary definitions (correct)\n\n> “Denote  \n> \\[\n> S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n> \\qquad\n> C_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}\\;(1\\le a\\le n).\n> \\]  \n> The column \\(C_a\\) contains exactly \\(n+1-a\\) points.”\n\n**Verification:** Straightforward counting; correct.\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Bound on points covered by sunny lines – correct\n\n> “A sunny line meets each vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point (its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\)).  \n> Hence, if \\(r_a\\) denotes the number of points of \\(C_a\\) covered by sunny lines, we have  \n> \\[\n> 0\\le r_a\\le k\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n> \\sum_{a=1}^{n} r_a\\le kn. \\tag{4.1}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- A non‑vertical line intersects each vertical line exactly once (though the intersection may be non‑integral; the bound “at most one point of \\(C_a\\)” is still valid).  \n- With \\(k\\) sunny lines, each column can receive at most \\(k\\) points from them, giving the stated inequalities.  \n\n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Description of non‑sunny lines – correct\n\n> “A non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n> * A vertical line can cover **all** points of a single column, but it cannot help any other column.  \n> * A horizontal line or a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contribute at most one point to each column.”\n\n**Verification:** Accurate classification and properties.\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Claim that a column with more than \\(k\\) points forces a vertical line – **Justification Gap**\n\n> “Consequently, if a column contains more points than can be supplied by sunny lines, i.e. if  \n> \\[\n> n+1-a>k,\n> \\tag{4.2}\n> \\]  \n> then at least one vertical line is indispensable for that column.”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The column has \\(n+1-a\\) points. Sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) of them, leaving at least \\((n+1-a)-k>0\\) points.  \n- These remaining points could be covered by several horizontal lines (or slope \\(-1\\) lines), each covering a distinct point of the column, without any vertical line.  \n- The argument that a vertical line is *indispensable* is therefore unsupported.  \n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Deriving a lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines – depends on the previous gap\n\n> “Condition (4.2) holds precisely for the columns  \n> \\[\n> a=1,2,\\dots ,n-k .\n> \\]  \n> Hence **at least** \\(n-k\\) vertical (hence non‑sunny) lines are required.  \n> Since the total number of lines equals \\(n\\), the number \\(N_{\\text{ns}}\\) of non‑sunny lines satisfies  \n> \\[\n> N_{\\text{ns}}\\ge n-k . \\tag{4.3}\n> \\]”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The set of columns with \\(n+1-a>k\\) is correctly identified as \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \n- However, the conclusion “at least \\(n-k\\) vertical lines are required” rests on the unjustified claim from step 8. Without that claim, we can only assert that **some** non‑sunny lines are needed, not necessarily vertical ones, and we cannot deduce the quantitative lower bound \\(n-k\\).  \n\n**Result:** This step inherits the **Justification Gap** from step 8; the inequality (4.3) is not proved.\n\n---\n\n### 10.  Argument for the extremal case \\(k+N_{\\text{ns}}=n\\) – built on the flawed bound\n\n> “If \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) then \\(n-k\\ge 2\\).  \n> Using (4.3) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> k+N_{\\text{ns}}\\ge k+(n-k)=n,\n> \\]  \n> with equality only when **exactly** the \\(n-k\\) columns \\(a\\le n-k\\) are covered by vertical lines and **no** horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) line is used. …”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The inequality follows from (4.3), which is unproved.  \n- The “equality only when” statement assumes that the only way to achieve the lower bound is to use vertical lines exclusively for those columns, ignoring the possibility of using horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) lines. This is not justified.  \n\n**Classification:** **Justification Gap** (the conclusion is not rigorously derived).\n\n---\n\n### 11.  Claim about covering the right‑most column – **Critical Error**\n\n> “The column \\(a=n\\) contains a single point \\((n,1)\\); therefore one sunny line must pass through \\((n,1)\\).  \n> Any line through \\((n,1)\\) and another point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a<n\\) has slope \\(\\frac{b-1}{a-n}\\). For the line to be sunny this slope must be different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n> The only way to avoid slope \\(-1\\) is to choose a point with \\(b\\neq n-a+1\\).  \n> But every point of the set \\(S_n\\) with \\(a<n\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n-a\\); hence \\(b=n-a\\) is the **largest** possible ordinate in column \\(a\\). The point \\((a,n-a)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\), whose slope is \\(-1\\).”\n\n**Analysis:**  \n- The correct bound for points in \\(S_n\\) is \\(b\\le n+1-a\\), not \\(b\\le n-a\\).  \n- Consequently the maximal point in column \\(a\\) is \\((a,n+1-a)\\), which lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), not on \\(x+y=n\\).  \n- The statement “every point … satisfies \\(b\\le n-a\\)” is false; for example \\((1,n)\\) has \\(b=n\\) while \\(n-a=n-1\\) when \\(a=1\\).  \n- Because the bound is wrong, the subsequent deduction that any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) must also contain a point with \\(b\\le n-a-1\\) is unsupported.  \n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** – the argument rests on an incorrect description of the set \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 12.  Final contradiction and conclusion – dependent on previous errors\n\n> “Consequently any sunny line that contains \\((n,1)\\) must also contain a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\le n-a-1\\). Such a point belongs to a column \\(a\\le n-1\\) that, by (4.2), already requires a vertical line for full coverage.  \n> Hence the sunny line woul